<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hay–Herrán Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1903</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hay–Herrán Treaty was signed in January 1903 by US Secretary of State John Hay and the Colombian chargé d'affaires Tomás Herrán. It would have allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to build a canal across Panama, then part of Colombia, in a zone leased for 100 years. The Colombian Senate rejected it in August 1903, and the United States then backed the Panamanian independence movement in November.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hay-herran-treaty.jpg" alt="Hay–Herrán Treaty" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles DeForest Fredricks · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hay%E2%80%93Herr%C3%A1n_Treaty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
